@3610[3]. In each plant lies hidden the endeavour to produce fruits or seeds; but this endeavour does not manifest itself until it has first produced all the means; namely, the branches, leaves, and flowers...

@8326. "Thou wilt plant them" (Exodus 15:17) = regeneration continually. To "plant" = to regenerate; for regeneration is circumstanced like plantation. Explained.

@9258[2]. The Prolific in plants corresponds to the good in man...

@--. As the Prolific in the seed produces a new tree, or a new plant.

@9272[3]. To "plant plants of delights" (Isaiah 17:10) = such things as favour the affections.

@9577. In the First Heaven they see... flowers and plants, etc.

@DLW 65[2]. Mediates are... plants, etc.

@AE 340[13]. "Plants" (Psalms 128:3) = truths.

@386[21]. To "plant them" (Psalms 107:37) = to receive them spiritually; that is, to understand them.

@405[15]. To "plant a vine" (Psalms 80:8) = to instaurate the Spiritual Church.

@458[6]. "Plantation" (Psalms 92:13) takes place in the interiors of man, where is the good of love and of charity; and "growth," in the exteriors, where is the good of life.

@654[17]. To "plant," etc. (Psalms 80:8) = instruction according to order...

@706[17]. To "plant vineyards" = the implantation of truth and its reception.

@724[11]. "Plants" (Psalms 144:12) = truths.

@727[3]. To "Vie planted" (Ezekiel 19:13) = to be instaurated.

@734[12]. To "plant a vineyard"- (Deuteronomy 20:6) = to instaurate the Church.

@840[5]. To "plant, and build" (Luke 17:28) = to confirm themselves in evils and falsities, and to live in them.

@DLove 8. By all things of the world, are meant plants, etc.

@DLove 8[2]. There is no tree, plant, etc., and nothing in a tree, plant, etc., which is not for use.

@DLove 10[3]. In the vegetable kingdom there are two universals; in one are the trees of fruit; and in the other are the plants of seeds...

@DLove 21. By vegetable forms are meant... plants, etc.

@DWis 12. 3. 4. Whether it be a tree with its fruit, or a plant with its seed.

@DWis 12. 5. By vegetative souls are meant trees and plants of every kind.

Plantation.; Arboretum.

@AC 2722[7]. A grove, or plantation, = the things which are of the rational things which are adjoined to doctrine and its Knowledges; for trees in general = perceptions; but, when predicated of the Spiritual Church, they = Knowledges...

@6832. All the small trees = scientifics; but the greater plantations = Knowledges and perceptions.

@10194[2]. Around those who are in truths, which are not from good... plantations sometimes appear; but unlovely and barren.

@10517[2]. The inhabitants of (the Third) Earth love plantations of trees, and an edifice from them... for trees and wood = goods.

Plaster.; Emplastrum. @TCR 134[5].

Plaster.; Gypsum. Gypseus.

@AC 5393[end]. There appeared a wall as it were of plaster, with sculptures... @SE 943.

Plaster, To.; Incrustare.
Plastering, A.; Incrustatio.
Plasterer.; Incrustator.

@AC 739[2]. "A wall plastered with what is unfit" (Ezekiel 13:10, 11) = fiction appearing as truth. @7553[3].

@DP 318[8]. Such are but plasterers of the wall.

@AR 442[3]. They live in... huts of rushes and reeds plastered over with lime.

@CL 78[3]. The houses were built of bricks, with beams between, and plastered over.

@231[2]. The light, by its flickering on the plastered walls, presented birds of night.

@TCR 519. They are like plastered walls, the plastering beautifully coloured, within which fly birds of night.

@797[3]. The walls of (Melancthon's) room appeared plastered with lime.

@--[6]. The former plastering and emptiness then returned.

@SE 245. Concerning Spirits who pervert holy things; [represented] by a plastering of what is unfit.

@--. They as it were plaster over holy things, and thus bring them to the perception of man.

@943. Such dwell within a wall... white, as if of plastered gypsum, with beautiful sculptures made of the gypsum.

@4721. A chamber plastered within with something snowy; built of brick work, but plastered... There was profanity there.

@4739. A large continuous building... of stone plastered with lime of a grey colour... Temples also of plastered stone...

@AE 237[5]. "Plastering" (Ezekiel 13) = application, and the derivative apparent confirmation.

@503[9]. "Those who plaster what is unfit" (Ezekiel 13) = those who confirm falsities, so that outwardly they appear like truths.

@644[25]. "The plastering of what is unfit" (Ezekiel 13) = the confirmation of falsity by means of fallacies, through which falsity appears like truth. @Coro 34[3].

Plastic.; Plasticus.

@CL 238[2]. This propagative or plastic force in seeds... and in souls... is from the conjugial sphere...

@AE 1201[3]. There is in everything spiritual a plastic force, wherever homogeneous exhalations are present in nature...

Plate.; Bractea.

@AC 9930. "Thou shalt make a plate of pure gold" (Exodus 28:36) = illustration from the Lord's Divine good. "A plate" = illustration... The reason "a plate" = illustration, was from its resplendence; for it was resplendent with gold upon Aaron's forehead...

@--[2]. As by "the plate" was signified illustration from the Lord's Divine good, it was called "the plate of the crown of holiness," and also "the crown of holiness." Illustrated. @AR 189[3].

@9932[2]. The reason "Holiness to Jehovah" was engraved on the plate of gold...

@AR 347[2]. As "the forehead" = love, it is said of the plate that... @AE 427[7].

@AE 272[11]. That "a crown" = Divine good from which is Divine truth, was represented by "the plate of gold"... which plate was also called "a crown," and "a coronet." Illustrated.

Plate.; Lamella.

@CL 182[end]. They gave them copper plates, on which were engraved some hieroglyphics.

@380[2]. Covered with glazed plates... like those called mica. @TCR 35[2].

Plate.; Patella.

@CL 14[2]. Dishes and plates loaded with food.

@16. Take each of you a plate... and instantly new plates appeared.

@SE 3135. A bunch of grapes upon a plate.

Platform. See <Pulpit-Suggestum>.

Plato.; Plato.
Platonist.; Platonicus.

